# Aviary - birdsong.
#
# A bird sings from its syrinx: two sources, one in each branch of the
# airway, whose folds start to swing once the pressure passes a threshold.
# Above them the trachea, a throat tuned to the note, and the beak. A held
# note sets a bird singing two octaves up, in time with the tempo.
#
# `pattern` is the song: a plain whistle (#0), chirps (#1), trills (#2), a
# warbled song in phrases (#3), a call answered in the gaps (#4), a chorus of birds
# taking turns, each its own song, pitch and distance (#5), slow falling coos with the last held (#6), or
# harsh caws in twos and threes (#7). `rate` is syllables a beat, `length` how much of each it sings,
# `sweep` how far each one slides (falling from above, or rising from below
# when negative). `two` lets the syrinx's other side sing at `interval`,
# `rasp` makes the folds slap, `breath` adds air, `throat` and `beak` shape
# it, `flock` adds birds and `space` puts them further off. Pressure blows
# harder; the mod wheel widens the sweeps.
#
# Forty-nine patches in seven families of seven, plainest first. Init is a
# chirp and comes first, so the chirps' tab comes before the whistles'.
#
#   whistle  held whistles, and coos: doves, a wood pigeon, owls
#   chirp    Init, chirps and peeps
#   trill    trills, a nightjar's churr, a wren
#   warble   songs in phrases: a skylark, lorikeets, a robin at dusk
#   call     two-note calls, hoots, and caws: crows, gulls, a jay
#   chorus   flocks: a dawn chorus, a rookery, a seabird cliff, a rainforest
#   kit      sixteen pads each: a scene on one track (the bird kit, a hedgerow,
#            a wood at dawn, a marsh, a night, a seashore, a rainforest)
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are
# numbered as in Aviary.h's MacroDest: #1 length, #2 sweep, #3 rasp, #4 two,
# #5 interval, #6 breath, #7 throat, #8 beak, #9 spread, #10 space, #12
# volume, #13 pitch (24 semitones a unit). Each sound gets the eight that
# move it, e.g. a whistle Slide, Air, Hollow, Open, Rasp, Duet, Far and Low;
# a caw Longer, Harsh, Arch, Air, Hollow, Open, Far and Low. A macro whose
# knob has no room left goes the other way under the opposite name (Solo,
# Shorter, Near, ...). A kit's macros reach the knobs every pad shares.
# Where a macro at full moves the level by more than 1.5 dB, a volume route
# takes it back out. Levels are read off an 8 s held note, on its loudest
# tenth, since a coo or a caw is mostly rests between calls.

machine Aviary
role lead

patch Init family=chirp note=60 range=48..72

patch "Pure Whistle" family=whistle note=60 range=48..72
  # A clear held note, sliding into it.
  set macro1 "Slide"
  set macro2 "Air"
  set macro3 "Hollow"
  set macro4 "Open"
  set macro5 "Rasp"
  set macro6 "Duet"
  set macro7 "Far"
  set macro8 "Low"
  mac1_d1  #2
  mac1_a1  0.15
  mac2_d1  #6
  mac2_a1  0.35
  mac3_d1  #7
  mac3_a1  0.35
  mac4_d1  #8
  mac4_a1  0.35
  mac5_d1  #3
  mac5_a1  0.4
  mac6_d1  #4
  mac6_a1  0.6
  mac6_d2  #12
  mac6_a2  -0.189
  mac7_d1  #10
  mac7_a1  0.45
  mac7_d2  #12
  mac7_a2  0.26
  mac8_d1  #13
  mac8_a1  -0.5
  mac8_d2  #12
  mac8_a2  0.278
  pattern        #0
  sweep          4 st

